#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined"}] :question/id #uuid "282add39-5184-589e-bb7b-b87a59cd41e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eead78a813f820cf12cbd05bd22b230b1753c7e4ebe1e77d2c1eb6b61c11f4ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"}] :question/id #uuid "fb401fbe-a718-5ea7-b598-139ef837b047" :question/math-academy-id "e-2" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0} f(x)$ for the function below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0ed78e2e640611d70263e17a76eb4a13e9e2816efba02494b53e540d38065d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "ab95d805-958c-5de3-99bd-41368cc57080" :question/math-academy-id "e-3" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09eb58fc-db0a-591c-80dc-ab3b7bc520c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513557} {:answer/id #uuid "22aa6597-ab52-5b75-99a4-6b7169a14c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513625} {:answer/id #uuid "5df8c413-c26f-5f42-ac67-136b2b31dc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513784} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9cd59c-78db-5f98-80ed-7eef9e41e3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513933} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b231a6-a623-5fb9-a9f6-14b416081d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a9cd59c-78db-5f98-80ed-7eef9e41e3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513933} :answer-field/id #uuid "14414f8e-6193-5fb6-baea-94b2b7d400fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9722b50e-b540-58f7-8d1d-e4a47908bdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-105944" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7defdf7c31a164fdbe702c1f5e87567f5da868d7530143493f9713ff88de98bc.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)$ for the function above." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\frac{3}{2}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(-\\frac{3}{2})$ is $0.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $-\\frac{3}{2},$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca6bb5d-b4ea-553b-853f-9ceddd8cfae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca6bb5d-b4ea-553b-853f-9ceddd8cfae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513562} :answer-field/id #uuid "471d69b4-8ecc-55fb-b5cf-d3a8ac996b27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5f74f57-aaaa-5a36-a7e2-f4e15eb6a61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-300752" :question/problem "![](images/93/93fc83d79679ca9f2b58729af1e3e87c2a01c19c12bc97670fbff398d63d061d.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76505ae0-b05a-5fec-9162-fea6c9089971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76505ae0-b05a-5fec-9162-fea6c9089971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513846} :answer-field/id #uuid "f555d267-91c8-5e05-8e23-98802f0b454c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c2e4638-f161-548b-867c-b6be12db26ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-335712" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c336dbdbda8ea15f5d2e86eb75493690d11ad0080c3ceeac9f71ab48cb63737b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a2d33a-8edf-5f9d-9762-1df5f4fdb217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0a2d33a-8edf-5f9d-9762-1df5f4fdb217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514160} :answer-field/id #uuid "04852450-d7a3-5aa8-bc86-7555a6eb7107" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87458c76-eabf-5005-ac30-a442db3ab7b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335759" :question/problem "![](images/22/22acfe14709e4992ddadf6d1d4fcd0e2a533c93573c32dd4134ec3498b0ea805.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function above.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $-3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(2)$ is $-2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00825b34-ca14-5ae2-ab19-c20bf17e8d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513527} {:answer/id #uuid "36a59ce0-10d0-5c8f-a5e5-89f4077f85c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513677} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f1fe47-ace4-5f56-b111-f3a8735fbf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514017} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f221f0-cc8f-5a04-b326-1e63cf89a6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514091} {:answer/id #uuid "d8534147-a200-5784-b2ea-387fd8f94bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00825b34-ca14-5ae2-ab19-c20bf17e8d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513527} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c4b582-3613-535f-9f83-090b0b7dae3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bebd627-ffbe-5f9e-9279-43f33938aacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-34924" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8b33f31c4b569da24aa8235421698859e74b144e783f0fb917a5f4b3679d834.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db872f0-8799-5efd-81e7-d8ed8388da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513743} {:answer/id #uuid "538b1e9d-53e0-5462-b056-10b1cbc26d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513763} {:answer/id #uuid "6c526a4b-1fc6-5714-8922-861633a05946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513826} {:answer/id #uuid "80be8d3f-0137-5d6e-9361-4b53f0963293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513867} {:answer/id #uuid "bba5df6a-0195-5eb0-a6c3-8cde93cd09f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80be8d3f-0137-5d6e-9361-4b53f0963293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513867} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf4a92b8-1b37-555c-b412-fdb7e36b861f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e220bff6-4f48-5639-8c40-6889828b8e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34927" :question/problem "![](images/67/6769c6590446a9599786758ba9cfaf17690f401d864b3ce5f2c3825d9ca432cd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb82a00-69d6-5e87-a076-068950ddd684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513658} {:answer/id #uuid "60822c2e-8f36-5580-9afd-b41c34d67685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513792} {:answer/id #uuid "60b10e04-f489-5160-a1fb-67046c009e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513793} {:answer/id #uuid "ba859d19-929d-5fdc-a7af-f4b2859004ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514026} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5088e7-09cb-53f4-abfc-2b5ef87f40ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b10e04-f489-5160-a1fb-67046c009e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513793} :answer-field/id #uuid "5697474b-eeff-52ef-8b60-0def9a73227e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1580d634-b7c9-566e-bd94-0e3785439c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-67531" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a45bf16dabedfdd0c8f4569289c56a5f89c127e240f57a8b9259429be86a9cde.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $2,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$"}]}
